    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d220">

                <pb facs="0220" n="208" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg208"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/503: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-23T13:00:00+03:00">September 23,
                            1939—1 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 23—7:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>595. The communiqué of the General Staff for September 22, published this morning
                    states that on September 22, the units of the Red <pb facs="0221" n="209"
                        xml:id="pg209"/> army occupied the town of Belostok<anchor n="11"
                        xml:id="d220-f1"/> and the fortress of Brest Litovsk<anchor n="12"
                        xml:id="d220-f2"/> and continued mopping up the remnants of the Polish Army
                    in the forest of Antust to the northwest of the town of Grodno.<anchor n="13"
                        xml:id="d220-f3"/> The communiqué continues that units of the Red army have
                    cleared out the remnants of the Polish Army and “officer groups” in the region
                    of Sarny and that in the region of Lwow<anchor n="14" xml:id="d220-f4"/> 6
                    Polish infantry divisions and 2 regiments of sharpshooters, headed by General
                    Langer, had surrendered. The communiqué concludes that in the period from
                    September 17 to September 21, 120,000 officers and men have been taken prisoner
                    with the capture of 380 cannon and 1,400 machine guns.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[For the note of protest made on September 30, 1939, by the Polish Government
                        against the Boundary and Friendship Treaty between Germany and the Soviet
                        Union signed at Moscow on September 28, 1939, see <ref
                            target="frus:frus1939v01/pg_462">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1939, volume I, page
                            462</ref>. The text of the treaty is in <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Nazi-Soviet Relations, 1939–1941</hi>, page 105; or, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Reichsgesetzblatt</hi>, 1940, Teil II, No. 1
                        (January 5, 1940), page 4, including the additional protocol of October 4,
                        1939; or, <hi rendition="#italic">Documents on German Foreign Policy,
                            1918–1945</hi>, Series D, volume VIII, page 167.]</p>
                </postscript>
                <postscript>
                    <p>[A statement by the Secretary of State was issued on October 2, 1939, wherein
                        it was declared that, although Poland had become the victim of force, the
                        United States continued to regard the Government of Poland as in existence.
                        For text, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>,
                        October 7, 1939, page 342.]</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
